 The dependency graph guides context selection during instantiation and repair. Without it, \tool{} lacks explicit links for selecting dependency-relevant statements and object-state summaries for prompting. For example, in \texttt{ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN b INT; SELECT b FROM t;}, the edge selects the schema update for the translation and repair of the \texttt{SELECT}. Summaries provide the content, while edges guide the selection.
 
-To isolate this benefit, we have added an ablation study in Section~IV (Evaluation). We compare four context-selection modes under the same LLM, knowledge base, source corpus, repair budget, and statement order: (1) \tool{}, which selects dependency predecessors through $\Delta$; (2) $\tool{}_{\textit{win}}$, which replaces dependency-based selection with the five most recent successfully translated statements while keeping the same object summaries, so that it differs from \tool{} only in whether the dependency graph is used; (3) $\tool{}_{\textit{all}}$, which provides all preceding successfully translated statements together with the complete source-side and target-side context; and (4) $\tool{}_{\textit{w/o-ctx}}$, which disables target-side context propagation. We measure execution validity at the statement level, the number of predecessor statements provided per statement, the proportion of provided predecessors that are dependency-relevant (i.e., connected to the current statement in $\Delta$), and input tokens per statement.
